{
  "note": "Neuron",
  "source": "9822fc6d7f95",
  "script": "f1a33a7fe519",
  "voice": "Jessica Gallagher - Clear and Neutral",
  "model": "eleven_v3",
  "fingerprint": "7054469826b4",
  "duration": 158.589,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Neuron",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Let's start with the neuron, which is the structural and functional unit of the nervous system.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 4.8
        },
        {
          "text": "It is an electrically excitable cell, specialised for receiving, integrating and transmitting information.",
          "start": 4.987,
          "end": 11.36
        },
        {
          "text": "It does this through two kinds of signal, namely electrical signals, in the form of action potentials, and chemical signals at synapses.",
          "start": 11.573,
          "end": 20.001
        },
        {
          "text": "Its main components are the cell body, the dendrites and the axon, and we'll take each one in turn.",
          "start": 20.24,
          "end": 26.88
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Cell Body (Soma)",
      "start": 27.716,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "To begin with, the cell body, also called the soma, is the metabolic and synthetic centre of the neuron.",
          "start": 27.716,
          "end": 34.516
        },
        {
          "text": "It contains the organelles, namely the ribosomes, the mitochondria and the endoplasmic reticulum, which are responsible for protein synthesis and energy production.",
          "start": 34.729,
          "end": 45.476
        },
        {
          "text": "The soma also integrates incoming signals, and it maintains the neuron's survival.",
          "start": 45.676,
          "end": 51.076
        },
        {
          "text": "Located within the soma is one important subcomponent, the nucleus.",
          "start": 51.256,
          "end": 56.516
        },
        {
          "text": "The nucleus contains the genetic material, that is the DNA, and it controls all cellular activity.",
          "start": 56.736,
          "end": 64.196
        },
        {
          "text": "This includes gene expression for the synthesis of neurotransmitters.",
          "start": 64.34,
          "end": 68.676
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Dendrites",
      "start": 69.512,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Now, let's turn to the dendrites, which are branched processes, shaped a little like trees, that extend out from the soma.",
          "start": 69.512,
          "end": 77.112
        },
        {
          "text": "They are the main receptive structures of the neuron.",
          "start": 77.288,
          "end": 80.712
        },
        {
          "text": "In other words, they receive incoming signals from other neurons at synapses, and they conduct those signals towards the cell body.",
          "start": 81.032,
          "end": 89.672
        },
        {
          "text": "Their extensive branching vastly increases the surface area available for synaptic input.",
          "start": 89.832,
          "end": 96.392
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Axon",
      "start": 97.228,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "That brings us to the axon, a single, elongated projection of the neuron.",
          "start": 97.228,
          "end": 101.708
        },
        {
          "text": "The axon conducts signals away from the soma, onwards to other neurons, muscles or glands.",
          "start": 101.928,
          "end": 108.428
        },
        {
          "text": "It carries action potentials over distances ranging from millimetres to over a metre.",
          "start": 108.748,
          "end": 114.428
        },
        {
          "text": "The axon has its own key subcomponent as well, known as the axon hillock.",
          "start": 114.728,
          "end": 120.428
        },
        {
          "text": "The axon hillock is the cone shaped junction between the soma and the axon.",
          "start": 120.688,
          "end": 125.948
        },
        {
          "text": "It acts as the neuron's spike initiation zone.",
          "start": 126.215,
          "end": 129.388
        },
        {
          "text": "In practice, it sums, or integrates, all of the incoming excitatory and inhibitory postsynaptic potentials.",
          "start": 129.708,
          "end": 137.548
        },
        {
          "text": "If the threshold is reached here, an action potential is generated.",
          "start": 137.895,
          "end": 142.348
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "In short",
      "start": 143.203,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "So, to bring it all together, the dendrites receive, the soma integrates, the axon hillock triggers, and the axon transmits.",
          "start": 143.203,
          "end": 151.843
        },
        {
          "text": "Keep that sequence in mind, as it captures how the neuron receives, integrates and transmits information.",
          "start": 151.971,
          "end": 158.563
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
